//? Title: Compare forecast accuracy with the Diebold–Mariano test
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Test whether two forecasts have the same expected loss, allowing for overlapping errors

Two forecasting methods produce errors \(e_{1t}\) and \(e_{2t}\) for the same targets. Under a loss \(L\), the Diebold–Mariano test asks whether the loss differential \(d_t=L(e_{1t})-L(e_{2t})\) has mean zero. Its statistic is \(\bar d/\sqrt{\hat V}\), where \(\hat V\) estimates the variance of \(\bar d\) from the autocovariances of \(d_t\). The test makes no assumption about how the forecasts were produced, only that \(d_t\) is covariance stationary.

Overlap is the complication for multi-step forecasts. Errors of optimal \(h\)-step forecasts follow an MA(\(h-1\)) process because consecutive forecasts share \(h-1\) future shocks, so the differentials are autocorrelated too. Diebold and Mariano therefore include autocovariances up to lag \(h-1\). In small samples that test over-rejects. Harvey, Leybourne and Newbold proposed scaling the statistic by \(k=\sqrt{(n+1-2h+h(h-1)/n)/n}\) and using a \(t_{n-1}\) reference, which is what R's `dm.test` and `diebold_mariano_test` do.

The example simulates two equally accurate 4-step forecasters whose errors share an overlapping AR(1) component, and compares the size of four versions of the test:
- one that ignores the overlap (\(h=1\) variance)
- the original normal-reference test
- the corrected test
- the corrected test with Bartlett weights, whose variance cannot be negative

It then traces the power as one forecaster becomes noisier.

```{cpp, id=dmtest, file="figures/diebold_mariano_power.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/diebold_mariano.hpp>

#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <random>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using dm::stats::DieboldMarianoOptions;
using dm::stats::DmVariance;

// h-step errors: a shared optimal AR(1) error (phi = .8) plus each forecaster's own
// MA(h-1) noise with standard deviations s1 and s2.
struct Errors {
    std::vector<double> e1, e2;
};

Errors simulate(std::size_t n, std::size_t h, double s1, double s2, std::mt19937_64& random) {
    std::normal_distribution<double> z;
    std::vector<double> eps(n + h), a(n + h), b(n + h);
    for (std::size_t t = 0; t < n + h; ++t) {
        eps[t] = z(random);
        a[t] = s1 / std::sqrt(double(h)) * z(random);
        b[t] = s2 / std::sqrt(double(h)) * z(random);
    }
    Errors out;
    for (std::size_t t = 0; t < n; ++t) {
        double common = 0, u1 = 0, u2 = 0, w = 1;
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < h; ++j, w *= .8) {
            common += w * eps[t + h - 1 - j];
            u1 += a[t + h - 1 - j];
            u2 += b[t + h - 1 - j];
        }
        out.e1.push_back(common + u1);
        out.e2.push_back(common + u2);
    }
    return out;
}

double upper_normal(double x) { return .5 * std::erfc(x / std::sqrt(2.0)); }

int main() {
    std::mt19937_64 random(99);
    const std::size_t h = 4;
    const int reps = 4000;
    std::println("| n | Ignore overlap | Original DM | Corrected (R default) "
                 "| Corrected, Bartlett |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::vector<double> fallback_share;
    for (const std::size_t n : {30u, 60u, 240u}) {
        int ignore = 0, original = 0, corrected = 0, bartlett = 0, fallbacks = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
            const auto e = simulate(n, h, .6, .6, random);
            const auto naive = dm::stats::diebold_mariano_test(e.e1, e.e2);
            const auto hln = dm::stats::diebold_mariano_test(e.e1, e.e2, {.horizon = h});
            const auto bart = dm::stats::diebold_mariano_test(
                e.e1, e.e2, {.horizon = h, .variance = DmVariance::bartlett});
            // Original test: undo the small-sample factor and use the normal reference.
            const double z = hln.statistic / hln.correction;
            ignore += naive.p_value < .05;
            original += 2 * upper_normal(std::abs(z)) < .05;
            corrected += hln.p_value < .05;
            bartlett += bart.p_value < .05;
            fallbacks += hln.horizon_used != h;
        }
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", n, ignore / double(reps),
                     original / double(reps), corrected / double(reps),
                     bartlett / double(reps));
        fallback_share.push_back(100.0 * fallbacks / reps);
    }
    std::println("\nThe acf variance was not positive, so the test fell back to h = 1, in "
                 "{:.1f}%, {:.1f}% and {:.1f}% of samples at n = 30, 60 and 240.",
                 fallback_share[0], fallback_share[1], fallback_share[2]);

    // Power at n = 120 as forecaster 2's own noise grows.
    std::vector<double> ratio, power_corrected, power_bartlett;
    for (double s2 = .6; s2 <= 1.21; s2 += .05) {
        int c = 0, b = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < 2000; ++r) {
            const auto e = simulate(120, h, .6, s2, random);
            c += dm::stats::diebold_mariano_test(e.e1, e.e2, {.horizon = h}).p_value < .05;
            b += dm::stats::diebold_mariano_test(
                     e.e1, e.e2, {.horizon = h, .variance = DmVariance::bartlett})
                     .p_value < .05;
        }
        ratio.push_back(s2 / .6);
        power_corrected.push_back(c / 2000.0);
        power_bartlett.push_back(b / 2000.0);
    }
    std::println("\nPower at n = 120 when forecaster 2's noise s.d. is twice forecaster 1's: "
                 "corrected {:.2f}, Bartlett {:.2f}.", power_corrected.back(),
                 power_bartlett.back());

    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(ratio, power_corrected, "Corrected (acf)", {26, 90, 147}, 2)
        .line(ratio, power_bartlett, "Corrected (Bartlett)", {176, 72, 43}, 2)
        .line({1, 2}, {.05, .05}, "5% level", {150, 154, 160}, 1)
        .title("Diebold–Mariano power, 4-step forecasts, n = 120")
        .x_label("Noise s.d. ratio, forecaster 2 / forecaster 1")
        .y_label("Rejection rate")
        .ylim(0, 1.25)
        .size(700, 400);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/diebold_mariano_power.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | Ignore overlap | Original DM | Corrected (R default) | Corrected, Bartlett |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 30 | 0.217 | 0.133 | 0.081 | 0.068 |
| 60 | 0.227 | 0.088 | 0.066 | 0.080 |
| 240 | 0.234 | 0.060 | 0.054 | 0.082 |

The acf variance was not positive, so the test fell back to h = 1, in 0.5%, 0.1% and 0.0% of samples at n = 30, 60 and 240.

Power at n = 120 when forecaster 2's noise s.d. is twice forecaster 1's: corrected 0.38, Bartlett 0.47.
\image(figures/diebold_mariano_power.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Rejection rate of the corrected Diebold–Mariano test at the 5% level for 4-step forecasts and n = 120, as forecaster 2's own noise grows relative to forecaster 1's. The acf (truncated) and Bartlett variances are compared.)
\alttext(Two rising curves start near the grey 5% line at ratio 1. The orange Bartlett curve sits above the blue acf curve throughout and reaches about 0.47 at ratio 2, against about 0.38 for the acf curve.)

Ignoring the overlap is the costly mistake. With 4-step forecasts, a variance built only from \(\hat\gamma_0\) misses the positive autocovariances of the loss differential, and a nominal 5% test rejects a true null 22–23% of the time. Unlike the other versions, this does not improve with more data. The original Diebold–Mariano test gets the variance right asymptotically but over-rejects in small samples (13% at \(n=30\)). The Harvey–Leybourne–Newbold correction brings it to 8% at \(n=30\) and close to nominal by \(n=240\). This is the reason it is the default.

Bartlett weights avoid the occasional negative variance of the truncated sum, which happened in 0.5% of samples at \(n=30\) and triggered the \(h=1\) fallback. They buy that with bias: they down-weight genuine autocovariances at lags 1 to 3. They therefore underestimate the variance of an MA(3) differential, and their size drifts upward to 8% as \(n\) grows. Their apparent power advantage in the figure comes from the same bias. For overlapping forecasts the truncated estimator with the small-sample correction is the better default, and Bartlett weights are a fallback for samples where its variance turns negative.

Even at \(n=120\), a forecaster with twice the idiosyncratic noise is detected only 38% of the time, because the shared error component dominates both losses. Forecast-accuracy comparisons need long evaluation samples, or a loss differential with less common noise, before a non-rejection means much.

The statistic, p-values for all three alternatives, both variance estimators, absolute and squared loss, and the fallback to \(h=1\) when the truncated variance is negative all match R's `forecast::dm.test`. `diebold_mariano_test_differentials` accepts differentials from any loss.

[Diebold and Mariano (1995)|https://doi.org/10.1080/07350015.1995.10524599] · [Harvey, Leybourne and Newbold (1997)|https://doi.org/10.1016/S0169-2070(96)00719-4]
